<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odo, Count of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1023–c. 1057</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Otto of Savoy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odo was Count of Savoy in the mid-11th century, a son of Humbert I White-Hands, founder of the house of Savoy. Around 1046 he married Adelaide, heiress of the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, bringing the family lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> on the Italian side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Their children included Amadeus II and Peter I of Savoy, and the dynasty later became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Odo, Count of Savoy" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abate Ferrero di Lavriano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oddone_di_Savoia_(XI_century).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
